.\" nftui(1) man page
.\" Hand-written using groff `man' macros so the page renders on every
.\" Linux distro (mdoc would need BSD-style mandoc).
.\"
.\" The OPTIONS section mirrors the flagDocs registry in flags.go; keep
.\" the wording in sync when adding or renaming a flag.
.\"
.\" Verify with: mandoc -Tlint man/nftui.1
.\" Preview  with: man -l man/nftui.1
.\"
.TH NFTUI 1 "June 20, 2026" "nftui 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nftui \- Terminal User Interface for Linux nftables
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nftui
[\fB\-\-table\fR \fIname\fR]
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIfile\fR]
[\fB\-\-read\-only\fR]
[\fB\-\-help\fR]
[\fB\-\-version\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B nftui
is an interactive terminal UI for browsing and editing the Linux
kernel's nftables firewall ruleset. It talks to the kernel directly
over netlink (no
.BR nft (8)
round-trip on the read or edit path), so changes apply atomically with
the same semantics as
.B nft
commands. The
.BR nft (8)
binary is used only for the
.B \-\-config
flag's startup ruleset apply and for a few targeted table operations
where round-tripping through the CLI is safer than reconstructing
kernel state.
.PP
.B nftui
requires the
.B CAP_NET_ADMIN
capability at runtime. Run with
.BR sudo ,
or grant the capability once with
.B "setcap cap_net_admin=ep ./nftui"
\&.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-\-table " " \fIname\fR
Restrict the tree to a single table (matched by name across all
families). Unknown names exit before the TUI starts with the list of
available tables. A name that exists in multiple families
(e.g.\&
.B filter
in both
.B ip
and
.BR inet )
returns every match.
.TP
.BR \-\-config " " \fIfile\fR
Apply the given nftables ruleset via
.BI "nft \-f " file
before the TUI starts. This
.B mutates
the running ruleset \(em the file may contain
.BR "flush ruleset" .
Resolved before
.B \-\-table
so the post-load kernel state is what
.B \-\-table
validates against.
.TP
.B \-\-read\-only
Disable every write path: no rule
.RB ( add / insert / move / delete / edit / save ),
no chain or table or set
.RB ( create / delete ),
no counter reset. Blocked keys dim out of the footer and a red
.B "[READ-ONLY MODE]"
marker rides in every main view's title bar.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print the full flag list with one-line descriptions and usage examples
to standard output, then exit
.BR 0 .
Invalid flags emit the same usage to standard error and exit
.BR 2 .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print
.BR "nftui <version>"
to standard output and exit
.BR 0 .
The version is injected at release-build time; a source build reports the
Go build-info module version, or
.B dev
for a plain
.BR "go build" .
.SH "KEY BINDINGS"
.SS "Main tree view (tables, chains, sets, named objects)"
.TP
.BR \(ua " / " k
move selection up
.TP
.BR \(da " / " j
move selection down
.TP
.B "Enter / \(-> / \(<-"
expand or collapse the selected table
.TP
.B F3
open the selected chain in the rule list view
.TP
.B n
create a new table
.TP
.B c
create a new chain on the selected table
.TP
.B e
edit the selected table or chain
.TP
.B s
create a new set on the selected table
.TP
.B d
delete the selected table, chain, set, or named object (with confirmation)
.TP
.B R
reset the selected counter or quota to zero
.TP
.B /
enter incremental name search. Type to filter; Enter or
.B \(da
cycle forward through matches,
.B \(ua
cycles back, Esc clears.
.TP
.B r
refresh the tree from the kernel
.TP
.B "q / Esc / Ctrl+C"
quit
.SS "Chain view (rule list)"
.TP
.BR \(ua " / " k ", " \(da " / " j
navigate rules
.TP
.B F3
view the selected rule (read-only detail)
.TP
.B F4
edit the selected rule
.TP
.B a
append a new rule at the end of the chain
.TP
.B i
insert a new rule before the selected rule
.TP
.B K
move the selected rule up by one position
.TP
.B J
move the selected rule down by one position
.TP
.B d
delete the selected rule (with confirmation)
.TP
.B /
enter inline filter. Type to narrow the visible rules by substring
match on verdict, condition keyword, or comment. While filter is
active,
.BR \(ua / \(da
navigate the filtered list, Enter or
.B F3
opens the selected rule,
.B F4
opens the editor, Esc clears the filter.
.TP
.B Esc
return to the tree
.TP
.B q
quit
.SS "Rule editor"
.TP
.B "F5 / F6"
previous / next tab
.TP
.B "Tab / Shift+Tab"
next / previous field within the current tab
.TP
.B F2
validate and apply changes through netlink
.TP
.B "Esc / F3"
return without saving
.TP
.B "q / Ctrl+C"
quit
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B sudo nftui
browse the live ruleset.
.TP
.B sudo nftui \-\-table filter
show only the table(s) named
.IR filter .
.TP
.B sudo nftui \-\-config examples/example\-nftables\-01.conf
load the manual-test fixture, then browse it.
.TP
.B sudo nftui \-\-read\-only
safe browsing \(em every write key is dimmed and inert.
.TP
.B sudo nftui \-\-config new.conf \-\-read\-only
safe browsing of a pre-loaded fixture.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
must include
.BR nft (8)
for the
.B \-\-config
flag to work; the runtime does not otherwise depend on the
.B nft
binary.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I examples/example\-nftables\-01.conf
The canonical manual-test fixture in the source distribution. Every
documented feature has a corresponding section in this file; the
fixture is verified with
.B "nft \-c \-f examples/example\-nftables\-01.conf"
on every release.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
normal exit (also for explicit
.BR \-\-help )
.TP
.B 1
runtime error inside the TUI (e.g.\& netlink connection failure
mid-session, or an
.B nft
binary failure during
.BR \-\-config )
.TP
.B 2
invalid command-line usage (unknown flag, missing required value, or
unknown
.B \-\-table
target)
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR nft (8)
.PP
Project documentation in the source distribution: README.md (install
and feature overview) and CHANGELOG.md (per-version release notes in
Keep a Changelog format).
